01.a|ABCFIJ|Build a representation table for INT0,BOOLfalse,NIL and REF(slot0,generation1). Derive equality and admissible operations; construct equal payload bits with different tags and reject arithmetic on Boolean values.|Equal payload bits imply equal language values or C struct bytes define guest semantics.|Tags distinguish values; reference equality compares identity without dereferencing; field access separately validates liveness.|Add a new numeric tag and specify mixed-operation rules before implementing coercion.|Separate representation, equality and dereference contracts.|A tagged C representation supplies no automatic exhaustiveness or type enforcement. Switch validation and programmer discipline implement the guest-language boundary.
01.b|BCDFIJ|Round-trip the literal CVM1 image through explicit little-endian encoding and test every truncation point with a canary destination. Include INT32_MIN and a valid raw container containing an unknown opcode.|Raw codec acceptance authorizes execution or native struct serialization is portable.|Raw codec may transport unknown instructions; verified load rejects them; every failure preserves destination and written length.|Decode on a changed data model and audit exact-width and CHAR_BIT assumptions.|Container validation and instruction validation are separate stages.|Converting the decoded unsigned bit pattern to a representable signed mathematical value avoids relying on an out-of-range signed conversion policy.
01.c|ACDFJ|Create independently malformed magic, descriptor count, contiguous-region, local/arity and length cases; state the earliest rejecting boundary. Test oversized lengths before any copy or pointer derivation.|A later verifier can safely repair an already out-of-bounds decoder.|Container bounds reject before publication; zero entry arity is enforced by verified entry rather than the raw transport contract.|Add an optional versioned section and define skip/reject behavior for old readers.|Validate lengths against remaining bytes before interpreting them.|Format evolution needs an explicit compatibility policy. Ignoring unknown bytes without a length/version contract can desynchronize parsing.
02.a|ABCFHJ|Derive a two-pass verifier: complete instruction-boundary discovery followed by reachable stack-height propagation per function. Prove each queued instruction is first discovered once and bound work/storage by code bytes and edges.|A single reachable walk can ignore malformed unreachable operands or unbounded recursive analysis is necessary.|Every instruction is structurally decoded; reachable height work terminates under the finite worklist invariant; unreachable stack effects remain a distinct policy.|Add an opcode with two branch successors and update both passes from one declared width/effect table.|Structural validity does not depend on reachability.|Instruction width changes affect boundary discovery before stack semantics. A shared table reduces inconsistency but must itself be verified against the opcode specification.
02.b|BCDFJ|Generate one malformed program for each interior target, cross-function branch, wrong call arity, local index and inconsistent join. Pair each with a nearest valid program and verify failure-atomic load.|Rejecting all branches is an acceptable fix for a bad-target bug.|Every invalid case rejects at the appropriate boundary while valid neighbors load; joins require equal heights and return requires one frame-relative value.|Add a loop backedge that disagrees only after a second path reaches the join.|Record incoming heights on edges, not just one visit order.|A verifier must not accept based on whichever predecessor arrived first. Worklist order should not change the verdict.
02.c|ACDFJ|Construct structurally accepted programs with an uninitialized local, wrong arithmetic tag, nonterminating balanced loop and cross-frame operand pressure. Identify the runtime check needed in each.|Verified bytecode is fully type-safe, terminating and resource-safe.|Structural acceptance coexists with runtime rejection or fuel stop; each limitation has a concrete counterexample.|Extend abstract interpretation with initialized-local sets and explain the changed lattice/join rule.|State exactly which facts the abstract state carries.|A stronger verifier requires a sound transfer and join argument. Adding one fact does not establish unrelated lifetime, callback or termination properties.
03.a|BCDFJ|Trace every stack transition for checked add/subtract/multiply/divide, including negative division, zero divisor and INT32_MIN/-1. Implement admission before the C operation and test exact neighboring valid results.|Guest arithmetic may inherit host signed-overflow behavior.|The defined guest result is representable or RANGE; -7/2 is-3; no invalid C arithmetic is evaluated.|Define a separately versioned wrapping-int32 guest and rederive conversions and tests.|Guest semantics are an explicit language decision.|Changing erroring arithmetic to wrapping is a language change, not a repair. The host implementation must realize the chosen semantics without its own UB.
03.b|ACDFJ|Construct four minimal programs separating TYPE,RANGE,STACK_LIMIT and VERIFY; keep result output as a sentinel and inspect diagnostic VM state after each failure.|All failed programs have interchangeable errors or verification bounds all live operand frames.|Each failure reaches its intended layer; runtime total stack pressure can exceed per-function relative heights; result remains unchanged.|Add a host-service error after valid arithmetic and preserve the distinction.|A minimal counterexample should fail for one reason.|Multiple simultaneous faults make error precedence ambiguous. State any intended precedence explicitly rather than relying on incidental implementation order.
03.c|BCDFGIJ|Test failure at each dispatch boundary while preserving the output result, and document which internal diagnostic fields may change. Reinitialize before independent replay and compare GCC/Clang optimized behavior.|Failure-atomic result means the entire VM rolls back or a failed run can be resumed without a contract.|Output stays unchanged; consumed fuel and diagnostic state may differ; independent replay starts from initialized state.|Design a resumable VM API and specify the additional continuation/state invariants.|Separate external publication from internal execution progress.|Rollback and resumability are stronger contracts than unchanged result output. They require explicit ownership and instruction-commit boundaries.
04.a|ABCFJ|Hand-trace recursive sum(5) with function identity, invocation identity, IP, local initialization and operand base at every call/return. Keep one caller temporary live across a nested call.|One function descriptor can store all recursive local state.|Each invocation has independent locals/IP/base; return restores the caller's operand boundary and preserves its live temporary.|Use mutual recursion with two functions sharing the same local slot numbers.|A slot index is relative to an invocation.|Static function metadata and dynamic invocation state have different lifetimes. Reusing one local array across calls corrupts recursive state.
04.b|BCDFGJ|Test direct calls with exact and wrong arity, frame capacity16 and a seventeenth call. Verify refusal before constructing an extra frame and use generated C call traces to show interpreter recursion is absent.|Guest recursion necessarily consumes one C frame per guest call.|The explicit VM frame limit is enforced; the interpreter dispatch loop does not recursively call itself; argument movement preserves order.|Add tail-call elimination under a new opcode contract and compare resource behavior.|Check all call admission conditions before committing the new frame.|Tail calls can reuse a frame only when caller continuation and ownership obligations permit it. The language-level result and debugging model must remain explicit.
04.c|ACDFHJ|Compare finite recursive execution, guest frame exhaustion and a separate bounded native stack experiment. Report guest frame bytes, operand bytes and actual C stack evidence independently.|FRAME_LIMIT is native stack overflow or raising the guest limit has no host-memory cost.|The failure categories differ; larger fixed frame arrays consume explicit memory and can change placement/stack requirements.|Move VM storage from an automatic object to caller-owned static storage and reassess lifetime/reentrancy.|Find where the Cvm object itself is allocated.|Avoiding recursive interpreter calls does not eliminate the storage cost of the VM state. Caller placement and concurrent instances matter.
05.a|BCDFJ|Scan explicit source spans containing embedded NUL, maximum-length names, duplicate functions/parameters and forward references. Resolve let initializers in the outer scope and bodies in the new scope.|strlen defines every input span or a let binding is visible in its own initializer.|Malformed spans and duplicate declarations reject; forward calls use collected signatures; unbound self-reference rejects while outer shadowing works.|Add nested lexical blocks with slot reuse and prove escaped values cannot refer to reused locals.|Separate signature collection from body resolution.|The bounded source language has no closures. Adding capture changes lifetime and representation, so slot reuse cannot inherit the old proof unchanged.
05.b|BCDFJ|Compile an outer x=2 with (+ (let x40 x) x), then independently decode emitted instructions and stack effects before executing. Compare against a tiny mathematical expression evaluator written separately.|Parser acceptance or one final result proves correct binding and code generation.|Result42 and outer x2 follow lexical scope; emitted bytecode also passes independent structural checks; malformed code cannot be hidden by an unexecuted branch.|Change to assignment inside the inner scope and predict which binding changes.|Draw the binding environment at each source node.|A shared parser/compiler oracle can share a defect. Independent semantic evaluation and bytecode inspection provide different checks.
05.c|BCDFGJ|Inject source-length, nesting, code-space and local-slot exhaustion with a preexisting canary program. Distinguish syntax/resource refusal from runtime errors and prove failed compilation preserves the old program.|Partial emitted code may be published because compilation eventually failed.|Every failure leaves the output program unchanged; compiler recursion and workspace bounds are reported separately from guest fuel.|Implement iterative parsing with an explicit bounded parse stack and compare failure behavior.|Stage the complete program before publication.|An iterative parser trades native call depth for explicit storage. Its bound and rollback behavior still require verification.
06.a|ABCFJ|Lower nested if/while expressions and annotate every patched absolute target and incoming stack height. Include an empty loop body result discarded at each iteration and a branch into an immediate mutant.|Patching a numeric offset is sufficient without instruction-boundary and join checks.|Targets land on valid instructions in the same function; both arms leave one expression value; loop backedges preserve height.|Add short-circuit Boolean operators and derive their distinct stack equations.|Write stack equations before emitting bytes.|Short-circuiting changes which effects execute. Treating it as eager arithmetic evaluation changes the language semantics.
06.b|ACDFJ|Compare set,do,if andwhile results with independent source-level traces. Use a side-effecting host counter only in the selected if arm and require while to return NIL.|C truthiness and host evaluation order automatically define the guest language.|Integer conditions reject; one if arm executes; set returns its assigned value; do discards the first result and while discards body values.|Introduce a break expression with a value and specify its effect on joins.|Guest control semantics must be written explicitly.|A new nonlocal control construct affects stack cleanup, roots and resource ledgers. It cannot be implemented safely by jumping to an arbitrary host label.
06.c|BCDFHJ|Run a terminating counter loop and a balanced infinite loop with exact fuel budgets immediately below, at and above completion. Count dispatched instructions independently of source iterations.|One source iteration costs one fuel unit or accepted compilation proves termination.|Fuel counts dispatches; the finite program completes only with sufficient budget; the infinite loop stops with FUEL and unchanged result output.|Change lowering to reduce opcode count and reassess fuel compatibility.|Inspect emitted control flow before predicting fuel.|Fuel is part of the execution contract. Compiler changes can alter the amount required even when source results are equivalent.
07.a|BCDFJ|Allocate pair handles, inspect fields and distinguish equal slot numbers with different generations. Force a slot to its final generation in a controlled valid model and require retirement instead of wrap.|Slot index is stable object identity across reuse.|Occupancy and exact generation are checked; exhausted generation cannot alias an old handle; output is unchanged on refusal.|Add an arena-instance identifier to distinguish reset lifetimes.|Generation scope must include the lifetime it identifies.|The current generation protocol is scoped to one initialized VM. Reset requires discarding old external handles unless a stronger identity scheme is introduced.
07.b|BCFHJ|Fill all16 arena slots with unreachable pairs, then attempt one more allocation. Compare with collection mode and report occupied, reachable and reclaimable counts separately.|Unreachable arena objects are automatically reusable.|Arena mode rejects the seventeenth allocation despite unreachable objects; collection mode has a different reclamation contract.|Use phased arenas and transfer a surviving graph to a new region.|Reachability and allocation policy are separate facts.|An arena provides bulk lifetime management, not tracing collection. Copying survivors requires preserving graph identity and handling cycles under a defined mapping.
07.c|ACFGJ|Specify ownership of VM state, host-held handles and explicit roots across reset and the transition from arena to collection mode. Demonstrate a host result that becomes vulnerable at the next allocation unless rooted.|Returning a handle implicitly roots it forever or reset preserves identity.|External references must be rooted before allocating operations and discarded across reset; root-stack ownership is balanced on failure.|Allow two embedding clients with independently scoped root leases.|Who owns the root record is as important as who holds the handle.|A root stack is a resource with capacity and lifetime. ISO C does not enforce balanced push/pop or prevent a client from retaining a stale handle.
08.a|ABCFHJ|Build a graph with cycles and aliases rooted separately in operand stack, initialized frame locals and explicit roots. Independently traverse the mathematical graph and compare marked objects; prove bounded worklist occupancy.|Every local slot is a root or recursive C traversal is required.|Only live initialized locations contribute roots; each object is enqueued once; aliases do not overflow the object-count worklist.|Add weak references under a new contract and separate tracing from weak processing.|Mark before enqueueing a newly discovered object.|The marked-set invariant bounds work despite cycles. Weak edges change reachability semantics and require a separate phase/policy.
08.b|BCDFGJ|Fill the heap, retain a child only as a C temporary and allocate its parent to force collection. Test both operands and failure of the second temporary-root push; verify balanced root restoration.|A C local is automatically visible to this precise collector or one rooted operand is sufficient.|Both operands survive collection; failed root admission restores the prior root count and publishes no parent; the missing-root mutant fails.|Add a helper that allocates between computing the two operands.|Every allocation can be a collection boundary.|Precise roots must cover all live temporaries across every allocating call. This is programmer discipline in C unless external tooling verifies the convention.
08.c|BCDFGJ|Exhaust GC budget at each root/edge examination and immediately before sweep. Snapshot occupancy/generations and verify no object is freed on incomplete marking; retry with fresh marks and sufficient budget.|Partial marking permits partial sweep or failed GC leaves a reusable complete mark set.|No failed collection sweeps; retry clears marks; stale references reject after actual reclamation; stop-the-world latency remains unbounded by guest fuel.|Introduce incremental marking and derive the write barrier required during mutation.|Sweep requires a complete reachability argument.|Incremental mutation invalidates the original static graph assumption. A barrier and phase invariant are new mechanisms, not a scheduling tweak.
09.a|BCFHJ|Record separate fuel,root/edge/sweep and host-invocation ledgers for programs exercising each mechanism alone and in composition. Define when a refused operation consumes its budget.|One budget counter represents all resource costs.|Each counter follows its own units and charge boundary; an ordinary infinite loop leaves GC/host budgets unchanged.|Change collection algorithm and recalibrate its work units without calling them cycles.|Resource units are contract choices.|Budget values are meaningful only with a specified charging scheme. Comparing different schemes as equal time is invalid.
09.b|BCDFGJ|Exhaust guest fuel,frames,operand slots,heap,explicit roots,GC budget and host budget independently with minimal programs. Require structured outcomes and unchanged result publication, then reinitialize before replay.|Any bounded refusal is a correct application result or all exhaustion is the same failure.|Each case reaches its intended resource boundary and preserves the declared result contract; a refused computation is not silently reported as success.|Compose two near-limit resources and identify which refusal occurs first under the specified order.|Avoid a second exhausted resource masking the intended one.|Minimal controls isolate mechanisms. Composition adds precedence and cleanup obligations that isolated tests cannot establish alone.
09.c|AFHIJ|Provide a bounded guest program invoking a host callback that may wait indefinitely under its own contract. Show why finite guest fuel cannot bound that call, verification,compilation or GC latency.|Instruction fuel is a wall-clock sandbox or deadline proof.|The counterexample identifies work outside dispatch accounting; the embedding contract needs a separate finite callback/deadline policy.|Replace blocking callback with an explicit pending/resume protocol and derive retained-state ownership.|A charged invocation can still contain unbounded work.|Accounting before entry limits invocation count, not duration. Asynchronous conversion introduces cancellation and lifetime requirements.
10.a|BCDFJ|Implement a service-ID allowlist for the existing integer callback, including absent service,wrong guest tag,denied budget and callback failure. Confirm denied calls produce no host side effect.|A numeric service ID is automatically authorized or a narrow signature makes native code safe.|Only allowed services execute with validated integer arguments; budget is charged at its declared boundary; arbitrary pointers/paths never cross.|Give two VM instances different service capability sets.|Capability policy belongs to the embedding adapter.|The VM cannot sandbox hostile native callback code. The callback remains trusted within its stated lifetime and resource contract.
10.b|BCDFGJ|Connect the accepted store using the fixed vm-result key and exactly four little-endian bytes. Test positive,negative and extreme values plus wrong stored length and transaction refusal.|A C integer struct dump is a portable store value or VM success certifies the store.|Encoding is explicit and length-checked; failures remain host outcomes; store and VM evidence retain separate source identities.|Add a version byte and migration/rejection policy for old values.|Define the stored format before the adapter code.|A format change is an integration contract change. A successful nominal round trip does not establish compatibility with old persisted data.
10.c|ACDFGJ|Inject a lost commit acknowledgment after persistence and another failure before publication. Return HOST_ERROR in both, recover the store and reconcile the actual value before retrying.|Callback error proves no external effect occurred.|The two errors can have different durable outcomes; blind retry is prohibited for non-idempotent effects; guest completion and persistence are separate.|Change the service from assignment to increment and add an operation-ID reconciliation protocol.|Error reporting and side-effect commitment are different boundaries.|Idempotent fixed assignment can mask duplicate execution. A non-idempotent service exposes why uncertainty must be represented explicitly.
11.a|BCDFIJ|Build the same VM core for Linux and Cortex-M4 with the hosted compiler excluded from firmware. Compare object imports,section placement and exact portable image bytes across builds.|A freestanding compile flag proves no runtime dependency or the source compiler belongs on the target.|The compiler remains host-only; generated helper references and final link closure are audited; identical wire bytes are checked independently.|Use another ABI/endian target and retain target-specific startup separately.|Source calls are not the complete dependency graph.|Compilers may emit memory/division helpers. Strict freestanding acceptance requires their original permitted implementations or elimination plus a final link audit.
11.b|BCDFIJ|Run one frozen bytecode image on Linux,the actual MPS2 AN386 emulator and the original kernel user route. Compare result/status/fuel contracts and retain each exact image/source/command transcript.|Cross-compilation or a host simulator result proves target execution.|Only actually run routes are marked executed; missing target capability remains pending; emulation proves its functional model,not board timing.|Change a host service adapter while retaining the same image and identify which result assumptions change.|The executable environment is part of the evidence identity.|Target equality requires equivalent service semantics and limits. A shared core does not erase adapter differences.
11.c|ADFIJ|Audit startup vectors,data/bss ranges,VM object placement,undefined symbols and link map for the strict target. Include one implicit-helper mutant in the dependency audit and reject an unexplained import.|No explicit libc call means no runtime dependency or an emulator validates physical timing.|Every linked dependency is accounted for; target startup and memory bounds are checked; physical claims stay separate.|Enable a different optimization/LTO setting and repeat the generated-code audit.|Inspect the final image as well as individual objects.|Optimization can introduce or remove helpers and alter stack use. A previous object's clean symbol table does not certify a new final link.
12.a|DFIJ|Freeze source text,compiler output,image hash,verifier verdict,VM build and result as a provenance chain. Insert a stale image from another source and require the dossier to reject the mixed claim.|A green runtime test proves the submitted source was compiled or complete runtime mastery.|Each boundary has its exact artifact and scope; mismatched provenance rejects; finite tests remain bounded evidence.|Have a second reader reproduce a selected chain from a clean learner export.|A result must be attributable to the artifact being assessed.|Reproducibility and independent mastery are separate. Author-run reference campaigns do not establish a learner's ability.
12.b|BCDFGJ|Challenge verifier,temporary roots and budget checks with independently constructed malformed programs and compiled mutants. For each killed mutant retain the smallest distinguishing valid/invalid pair and explain the mechanism.|Mutation score alone proves correctness or a crash proves a sandbox escape.|The evidence shows the specific violated property; no privilege or arbitrary-code-execution claim follows from a crash; untested mutations remain open.|Combine an accepted verifier case with a host-lifetime defect to test boundary composition.|A useful mutant changes a semantic obligation,not just an expected constant.|Visible mutants are guided regression controls. An unrevealed defect with independently established acceptance is needed for independent diagnosis assessment.
12.c|BCDFIJ|After at least seven days, change a capacity or add a bounded host service with a distinct failure contract. Derive revised invariants and acceptance before consulting the old implementation; submit a fresh dossier.|Remembering the old code demonstrates transfer under changed assumptions.|The new resource/lifetime/failure proof and tests address the changed mechanism; the assessment records whether the variant was truly withheld.|Compose the VM service with an asynchronous store adapter and specify continuation ownership.|List which old arguments rely on the assumption being changed.|Transfer may require a new representation or protocol. Passing the original suite cannot close the new integration obligations.
